{
  "requirement": "A definition glob MUST match a complete full name. `*` MUST match zero or more characters, including dots; all other valid name characters MUST match literally. An empty glob MUST be invalid. Every definition selection MUST use this matching rule, including requirements evidence inputs, CLI definition filters, and the authoring `references` tool, and MUST match against both requirements and named examples.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests cover every obligation in `requirements.references.definition_globs`. They assert outcomes, not just execution.\n\n**Matching rule (unit level)**\n- `src/definition_glob.rs#mod[tests]/fn[wildcard_crosses_dot_segments_and_matches_the_whole_name]` checks that `*` crosses dots: `foo.*.bar` matches `foo.x.y.bar`.\n- It checks whole-name anchoring: no match for a prefix (`prefix.foo.x.bar`) or a suffix (`foo.x.bar.suffix`).\n- It checks that other characters are literal: `.` is not a wildcard (`foo.bar` does not match `fooxbar`), and `_` and `-` match only themselves.\n- `foo.bar.*` does not match `foo.bar`, because the literal dot is still required.\n- `validate(\"\")` is an error, and so is `validate(\"foo[bar]\")`.\n\n**Zero-or-more characters**\n- `tests/commands.rs#fn[commands_report_invalid_arguments_and_unmatched_selectors]` runs `status requirements.*ready`. It asserts that both `requirements.ready` (zero characters matched) and `requirements.xready` (one character matched) appear, and that `requirements.pending` does not.\n\n**Empty and invalid globs**\n- The unit test above rejects the empty glob.\n- `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirements_inputs_track_matched_declarations_in_full_name_order]` asserts that evidence inputs with `\"\"` or `foo.[a].bar` produce errors (\"definition glob must not be empty\", \"unsupported character '['\").\n- The CLI test above asserts \"invalid definition glob\" for `info [` and `status [`.\n- `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[references_list_names_and_flattened_sites_without_recording_inputs]` asserts that the `references` tool errors on `\"\"` and on `bad name`.\n\n**Every selection uses the same rule**\n- Evidence inputs: the `evidence.rs` test checks that `foo.*.bar` matches both `foo.a.bar` and the dotted `foo.a.b.bar`. It also checks the add, remove and change cases, and that `foo.*.none` matches nothing.\n- CLI filters: the `commands.rs` test covers `status` and `info`, and `tests/definitions.rs#fn[selections_match_requirements_and_examples_but_never_named_definitions]` checks `rules.*` through both. The verbose-status test checks the `rules.case` and `rules.unlinked` selections.\n- Authoring `references` tool: the `tools.rs` references test uses `requirements.*`. The `requirements` read test in `tools.rs` uses `requirements.*` through `habeas`. The ripgrep and ast_grep `referencing` tests use globs too.\n\n**Both requirements and named examples**\n- The `tools.rs` `requirements` read test asserts that `requirements.*` returns `requirements.case` (an example) and `requirements.ready`, and nothing for the unreferenced definition.\n- The references-tool test lists both kinds.\n- `tests/definitions.rs` asserts that `rules.*` selects the requirement and the example but not the definition.\n- The same tests check that an exact definition name selects nothing.\n\nI found no scenario in the text that the tests leave unchecked.",
  "references": "e33afeaa4a252d265fbc645047b6ff36bea7de48291c4c2dd1b57ee30dd597e3",
